---
import ZetaLayoutV2 from "../../../sites/zeta/ZetaLayoutV2.astro";
---

<ZetaLayoutV2
	lang="en"
	title="Zeta — The Agentic Development Environment"
	description="Zeta is an ADE (Agentic Development Environment): one workspace that tiles real terminals, plus the tools that live inside it — a coding agent, an editor, a terminal IDE, a local web workbench, and a file manager. Linux-first, Windows supported, mouse-first."
	active="home"
>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="container hero-grid">
			<div class="hero-copy">
				<div class="kicker">Agentic Development Environment · by Linxira-OS</div>
				<h1>One terminal.<br>The <span class="accent">whole workbench.</span></h1>
				<p class="lede">
					<strong>Zeta</strong> is an <strong>ADE</strong> (Agentic Development Environment), not just a coding
					agent. It is also <strong>a workspace</strong>: tabs, tilable real-terminal panes, and a fully
					clickable menu bar — with four entry points: <strong>zetawork</strong> (workbench, GUI + terminal),
					<strong>zetacode</strong> (coding agent), <strong>zetaide</strong> (terminal IDE) and
					<strong>zeta serve</strong> (local web workbench). Linux is the primary platform; Windows is supported.
				</p>
				<div class="cta-row">
					<a class="cta primary" href="#install">Get started</a>
					<a class="cta ghost" href="/zeta/en/features/">See features →</a>
					<a class="cta ghost" href="https://github.com/Linxira-OS/linxira-zeta/releases/latest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Desktop app</a>
				</div>
			</div>

			<!-- Pure-CSS workbench mock: menu bar / tabs / panes with pages / widget panes / status -->
			<div class="term" role="img" aria-label="Zeta workbench mock: menu bar, tabs, terminal panes and widget panes">
				<div class="term-bar">
					<span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span>
					<span class="title">zeta — ~/projects/linxira</span>
				</div>
				<div class="term-menu">
					<span>Files</span><span>Edit</span><span>View</span><span class="on">Panes</span><span>Tools</span><span>Settings</span>
				</div>
				<div class="term-tabs">
					<span class="tt on">1 work</span><span class="tt">2 docs</span><span class="plus">＋</span>
				</div>
				<div class="term-panes">
					<div class="pane">
						<div class="pane-h on"><span class="ph-t">● zetacode</span><span class="ctl">+ ↔ ↕ ✕</span></div>
						<div class="out">
							<div><span class="prompt">❯</span> zetacode</div>
							<div><b>▸ planning</b> refactor: pane layout</div>
							<div><span class="ok">✓</span> 3 files patched, tests green</div>
							<div><span class="prompt">❯</span> <span class="cur"></span></div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="col">
						<div class="pane">
							<div class="pane-h"><span class="ph-t">🍅 pomodoro</span><span class="ctl">+ ✕</span></div>
							<div class="widget"><span class="big">24:31</span><span class="lbl">focus · 2nd round</span></div>
							<div class="widget"><span class="lbl">today 6 ✓ / 0 ✗</span></div>
						</div>
						<div class="pane">
							<div class="pane-h"><span class="ph-t">📋 clipboard</span><span class="ctl">+ ✕</span></div>
							<div class="clip"><span class="n">03</span> src/layout.rs</div>
							<div class="clip"><span class="n">02</span> bun run check:ts</div>
							<div class="clip"><span class="n">01</span> zeta doctor</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="term-status">
					<span><span class="st-ok">●</span> shell: bash</span><span>3 panes · 2 tabs</span><span>zetawork 1.3.1</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="ade">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">00</div>
				<div>
					<h2>An ADE, not an IDE</h2>
					<div class="sub">Zeta is an <b>ADE</b> (Agentic Development Environment). A classic IDE is built around a human writing code — editor, completion, debugger. An ADE is built around <b>agents doing the work</b>: the workspace, terminals and editor are the agent's surface, and you set goals, review, and take over when it matters.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>Agent-first</h3><p><code>zetacode</code> reads and writes files, runs commands and tests, and spawns sub-agents right inside the workspace — not a chat box bolted onto an IDE.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>You still get an IDE</h3><p>When you want to edit by hand, open <code>zetaide</code> (a terminal IDE): editor, LSP and project views in the same workspace, no second editor needed.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Runs a local server</h3><p><code>zeta serve</code> starts the web workbench on your own machine (Next.js + REST gateway); the browser and the desktop shell share one workspace. Nothing goes through the cloud.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>One workspace, four entry points</h3><p>GUI (<code>zetawork</code>), coding agent (<code>zetacode</code>), terminal IDE (<code>zetaide</code>) and web workbench (<code>zeta serve</code>) share one pane tree and one persistent session store.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="suite">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">01</div>
				<div>
					<h2>One suite, three commands, one local server</h2>
					<div class="sub">Every tool runs standalone with its own --help; the workspace puts them under one roof. <code>zeta serve</code> additionally starts a local web workbench from the same package.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="suite">
				<a class="suite-card hero-card" href="#install">
					<span class="pkg">@linxiraos/main</span>
					<div><span class="cmd">zetawork</span><span class="alias">workbench · GUI + terminal</span></div>
					<p>The workspace: a nested-terminal workbench. Tabs, a pane tree, page stacks, a menu bar, widget panes — written in Rust, mouse-first.</p>
					<div class="foot">Bare <b>zeta</b> prints these three names and exits</div>
				</a>
				<a class="suite-card" href="#install">
					<span class="pkg">@linxiraos/zeta</span>
					<div><span class="cmd">zetacode</span><span class="alias">coding agent</span></div>
					<p>Coding agent CLI: subagents, plan mode, LSP/DAP, hindsight memory, hashline editing, and time-travel rules out of the box.</p>
					<div class="foot">One package, one bin: no aliases, no space forms</div>
				</a>
				<a class="suite-card" href="#install">
					<span class="pkg">@linxiraos/ide</span>
					<div><span class="cmd">zetaide</span><span class="alias">terminal IDE</span></div>
					<p>Terminal IDE (TermIDE, vendored Rust): a near-graphical engineering experience in the terminal. Its built-in editor is the <code>@linxiraos/editor</code> payload, spawned by path — it has no bin of its own.</p>
					<div class="foot">TermIDE (vendored Rust) inside your terminal</div>
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="workspace">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">02</div>
				<div>
					<h2>The workspace is mouse-first</h2>
					<div class="sub">Every control is efficiently clickable; keyboard shortcuts are accelerators, not prerequisites.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="shot-strip" style="margin-bottom: 1.2rem;">
				<button class="shot wide" type="button"><img src="/assets/zetawork-gui-editor.png" alt="zetawork: project tree, editor with diff, and git status in the right rail" loading="lazy"></button>
			</div>
			<p class="install-note" style="margin: 0 0 1.6rem;">Rendered offscreen from the shipped shell — not a mockup. Project tree on the left, terminal and editor panes in the middle, git and usage on the right. Click to enlarge.</p>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>Tabs and the pane tree</h3><p>Split any pane left/right or top/bottom; a weighted pane tree keeps proportions exact. Each pane also keeps a stack of pages — several terminals in one pane, nothing closed.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>Fully clickable</h3><p>Menu bar, tab bar, pane title controls (+ ↔ ↕ ✕), and page tabs all respond to the pointer: switching, splitting, closing, and layout repair are mouse operations.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Widget panes</h3><p>A clock with a clickable calendar, a 🍅 pomodoro timer, and a 📋 clipboard history run as native panes — tools at hand, never leaving the terminal.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>Suite-aware</h3><p>The Tools menu knows the suite: a missing tool installs itself into the pane that asked for it. <span class="keycap">zeta doctor</span> checks every tool and prints the exact fix.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="agent">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">03</div>
				<div>
					<h2>A coding agent, out of the box</h2>
					<div class="sub">zetacode is built on the OMP runtime: core capabilities are built-in defaults, not optional extensions.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>The full tool surface</h3><p>File read/write, search, patching, shell, git, task delegation, and structured tool output — heavy work runs on native Rust primitives.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>Sessions are trees</h3><p>Branching history: resume from any node. Terminal, browser, and desktop share one persistent session.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Project tracking</h3><p>Turn on <span class="keycap">/tracking</span> and the agent writes milestones and key decisions to tracking docs — kept across sessions, browsable in the UI.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>Extensible to the bone</h3><p>TypeScript custom commands, a command marketplace, skills, MCP, full TUI hooks — the agent can customize itself on the spot.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="install">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">04</div>
				<div>
					<h2>One command to the workbench</h2>
					<div class="sub">Install the workspace; the rest of the suite self-installs on demand — or run zeta doctor for a full checkup.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="install">
				<div class="tabs">
					<button class="tab active" type="button" data-tab="npm">npm</button>
					<button class="tab" type="button" data-tab="bun">bun</button>
					<button class="tab" type="button" data-tab="linux">Linux</button>
					<button class="tab" type="button" data-tab="windows">Windows</button>
					<span class="tab-ind" id="tabInd" aria-hidden="true"></span>
				</div>
				<div class="code" data-pane="npm">
					<span class="cmd"><span class="prompt">$</span><span class="type" data-typing="npm install -g @linxiraos/main">npm install -g @linxiraos/main</span></span>
					<button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="npm install -g @linxiraos/main" data-done="Copied">Copy</button>
				</div>
				<div class="code" data-pane="bun" hidden>
					<span class="cmd"><span class="prompt">$</span><span class="type" data-typing="bun add -g @linxiraos/main">bun add -g @linxiraos/main</span></span>
					<button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="bun add -g @linxiraos/main" data-done="Copied">Copy</button>
				</div>
				<div class="code" data-pane="linux" hidden>
					<span class="cmd"><span class="prompt">$</span><span class="type" data-typing="curl -fsSL https://linxira-os.github.io/install.sh | sh">curl -fsSL https://linxira-os.github.io/install.sh | sh</span></span>
					<button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="curl -fsSL https://linxira-os.github.io/install.sh | sh" data-done="Copied">Copy</button>
				</div>
				<div class="code" data-pane="windows" hidden>
					<span class="cmd"><span class="prompt">PS&gt;</span><span class="type" data-typing="irm https://linxira-os.github.io/install.ps1 | iex">irm https://linxira-os.github.io/install.ps1 | iex</span></span>
					<button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="irm https://linxira-os.github.io/install.ps1 | iex" data-done="Copied">Copy</button>
				</div>
				<p class="install-note">
					Then: <code>zetawork</code> opens the workspace, <code>zetawork doctor</code> checks the whole suite.
					Coding agent alone: <code>npm install -g @linxiraos/zeta</code> (zetacode).
					Slow mirror? Try <code>--registry=https://mirrors.cloud.tencent.com/npm/</code>.
					Updating: <code>zetacode update</code> self-updates the CLI; reinstall anytime with the script above.
					Desktop build (the zetawork GUI only; zetacode and zetaide live in the terminal): Windows <code>.zip</code>, Arch <code>.pkg.tar.zst</code>, <code>.AppImage</code> — on <a href="https://github.com/Linxira-OS/linxira-zeta/releases/latest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Releases</a>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="webui">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">05</div>
				<div>
					<h2>The zetawork GUI</h2>
					<div class="sub">Real captures of the shipped shell: editor with diff, the total panel with project management, and the provider settings. The GUI owns three settings panes the terminal does not (web, coding-agent, provider settings) and an embedded PDF viewer. Click a screenshot to enlarge.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="shot-strip">
				<div class="shot-track">
					<div class="shot-group">
						<button class="shot" type="button"><img src="/assets/zetawork-gui-editor.png" alt="zetawork GUI: editor, diff, and git status" loading="lazy"></button>
						<button class="shot" type="button"><img src="/assets/zetawork-gui-total.png" alt="zetawork GUI: total panel and project management" loading="lazy"></button>
						<button class="shot" type="button"><img src="/assets/zetawork-gui-settings.png" alt="zetawork GUI: provider settings" loading="lazy"></button>
					</div>
					<div class="shot-group" aria-hidden="true">
						<button class="shot" type="button"><img src="/assets/zetawork-gui-editor.png" alt="" loading="lazy"></button>
						<button class="shot" type="button"><img src="/assets/zetawork-gui-total.png" alt="" loading="lazy"></button>
						<button class="shot" type="button"><img src="/assets/zetawork-gui-settings.png" alt="" loading="lazy"></button>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="lightbox" id="lightbox" hidden>
				<img id="lightboxImg" src="" alt="Enlarged zetawork screenshot">
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="modes">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">06</div>
				<div>
					<h2>Four run modes</h2>
					<div class="sub">One agent, many interfaces — from the full TUI to scripts, event streams, and programmatic embedding.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="modes">
				<div class="mode"><div class="mname"><span class="dot"></span>Interactive</div><p>The full TUI: panels, previews, tool cards.</p></div>
				<div class="mode"><div class="mname"><span class="dot"></span>Print / JSON</div><p><code>zetacode -p "query"</code> prints and exits; <code>--mode json</code> streams events.</p></div>
				<div class="mode"><div class="mname"><span class="dot alt"></span>RPC</div><p>A JSON protocol over stdin/stdout for non-Node integrations.</p></div>
				<div class="mode"><div class="mname"><span class="dot alt"></span>SDK</div><p>Embed the agent as a library and own its lifecycle.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="providers">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">07</div>
				<div>
					<h2>15+ providers, hundreds of models</h2>
					<div class="sub">Switch anytime in-session with <span class="keycap">/model</span>.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="tag-row">
				<span class="tag hot">Anthropic</span><span class="tag hot">OpenAI</span><span class="tag hot">Google</span>
				<span class="tag">Azure</span><span class="tag">Bedrock</span><span class="tag">Mistral</span>
				<span class="tag">Groq</span><span class="tag">Cerebras</span><span class="tag">xAI</span>
				<span class="tag">Hugging Face</span><span class="tag">Kimi</span><span class="tag">MiniMax</span>
				<span class="tag">NVIDIA</span><span class="tag">OpenRouter</span><span class="tag">Ollama</span>
				<span class="tag">More…</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="bots">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">08</div>
				<div>
					<h2>Drive Zeta from chat apps</h2>
					<div class="sub">Plug zetacode into WeChat, Feishu, and Telegram — ask, dispatch tasks, and receive results where you already talk.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="tag-row">
				<span class="tag hot">WeChat</span><span class="tag hot">Feishu</span><span class="tag hot">Telegram</span>
				<span class="tag">One bot interface</span><span class="tag">More platforms coming…</span>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="linux">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">09</div>
				<div>
					<h2>Native on Linux, not wrapped</h2>
					<div class="sub">Linux is the primary platform (Linxira OS, Arch-based); heavy work runs on native engines, and terminal and desktop are deeply integrated.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>Native engines</h3><p>Text, regex, grep, and diff go to Rust primitives; the workbench itself is a native Rust binary.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>The terminal is home</h3><p>OSC 8 hyperlinks, native PTY management; the whole suite is terminal-born and works over SSH.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Desktop bridge</h3><p>Open local editors and file managers safely from the Web UI: HMAC gateway tokens, target allowlists, workspace containment.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">04</div><h3>One command to land</h3><p>npm / Bun global install, or <code class="keycap">curl … install.sh | sh</code>; the desktop build ships as Arch <code>.pkg.tar.zst</code>, AppImage, and a Windows zip.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="block" id="identity">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-head">
				<div class="num">10</div>
				<div>
					<h2>A first-class citizen of Linxira OS</h2>
					<div class="sub">Zeta is built by the Linxira OS team — a Linux distribution for research workstations. On Linxira OS, Zeta is not just another package.</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-table">
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">01</div><h3>First-class identity</h3><p>Zeta will run under its own system identity (a dedicated UID) as a system-level citizen — not a process parasitic on some user session.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">02</div><h3>OS-enforced permissions</h3><p>Permission boundaries are enforced by the OS: Zeta declares policy, the system enforces it, changes need human approval — overreach is refused at the system layer.</p></div>
				<div class="grid-item"><div class="idx">03</div><h3>Private by default</h3><p>No access to personal data by default: private home files and other users' data are invisible to Zeta; every action is logged and auditable.</p></div>
			</div>
			<div class="cta-row" style="margin-top: 1.8rem;">
				<a class="cta ghost" href="/en/">About Linxira OS →</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</ZetaLayoutV2>
